  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb couldn't hold it together, and the Giants couldn't make anything happen when runners were in scoring position.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed from the first inning onward, chasing a five-run deficit after Padres jumped to 3\u20130 in the opening frame. The bats touched ball fourteen times with runners in scoring position but converted only twice into runs. Inning four was the real killer. Four at-bats with runners in position, three left stranded, and the Giants never recovered from that silence. Webb gave up six earned runs over five innings while striking out seven, a solid-enough outing undermined by early damage and a lineup that couldn't build pressure back. The bullpen worked cleanly after that: Wilkinson threw three scoreless, Harris threw one more clean. But two runs scored is still two runs scored, and clean relief doesn't matter if the offense can't climb out of a hole it dug itself into. Nine hits total. Nine strikeouts. This is what inconsistency looks like: broad enough contact to suggest a capable lineup, but nowhere near sharp enough when timing matters most.",
    "what_to_watch": "This team doesn't fail because it gives up. It fails because it can't seize the one opening it gets. They had real chances to shrink the margin and stayed flat. The formula didn't break today; the execution did. Fourteen runners in position. Two runs to show.",
